<!--
    GRTubeYou: the player's look, changed through resources only.

    Nothing here alters behaviour. These are the same attributes leanback's overlay already
    reads, re-declared with quieter values, so no layout and no code had to change - which is
    what keeps this an improvement to the existing player rather than a second UI built on top
    of it.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
        The timeline.

        The bar itself goes from 4dp to 3dp, and the played part from 6dp to 5dp. On a 1080p
        frame that is the difference between a bar and a rule: the old pair was drawn thick
        enough that the coloured chapter segments read as separate objects sitting on the line
        rather than as parts of it.

        The radius shrinks in step with the played part, which is what keeps its end round
        instead of turning into a lozenge - the same value that smoothed the line would flatten
        the cap.

        The 28dp bar height is NOT touched. That is not decoration: it is the height the row
        occupies and therefore how easy the bar is to grab from a sofa, and it is why the row
        has padding around a 3dp line in the first place. Making the line thinner does not
        change any of that.
    -->
    <dimen name="lb_playback_transport_progressbar_height">28dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="lb_playback_transport_progressbar_bar_height">3dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="lb_playback_transport_progressbar_active_bar_height">5dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="lb_playback_transport_progressbar_active_radius">5dp</dimen>

    <!--
        The height of the two readability gradients.

        These cannot be percentages: AAPT rejects a percentage for layout_height, so the bands
        are sized absolutely. On the usual Android TV panel - 1080p at xhdpi, which is 540dp
        tall - 100dp is a fifth of the screen and 170dp a bit under a third, which is the
        proportion they were chosen for. On a 720p panel they cover proportionally more, and
        that is the safe direction to err in: both gradients end in fully transparent, so the
        worst case is a picture that is a little more gently darkened at the edges than
        intended, not a band with a visible line across it.
    -->
    <dimen name="grtubeyou_player_scrim_top_height">100dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="grtubeyou_player_scrim_bottom_height">170dp</dimen>

    <!--
        The focus and ripple fill lives in colors.xml, not here: the app already overrides
        lb_control_button_color, and a second declaration of the same resource in another file
        is a duplicate-resource build failure rather than a preference. See that file.
    -->

</resources>